meta-s6
=======

This layer provides recipes for the skarnet.org software suite, centred
on the s6 process supervision suite, together with the integration
needed to boot a Yocto Project / OpenEmbedded image with s6-linux-init
as init and either s6-rc or sysvinit-style initscripts as the service
manager.

Contents
========

  * Recipes for the skarnet.org packages: skalibs, execline, s6, s6-rc,
    s6-frontend, s6-linux-init, s6-dns, s6-networking,
    s6-portable-utils, s6-linux-utils, mdevd, nsss, utmps, dnsfunnel,
    shibari, smtpd-starttls-proxy, tipidee, bcnm and apaste.

  * libretls (libtls on top of OpenSSL), used by s6-networking for its
    TLS support and not available in other common layers.

  * classes-recipe/skarnet.bbclass: shared configure/compile/install
    logic for the skarnet build system.

  * classes-recipe/s6-rc.bbclass: packages s6-rc service definitions,
    written as TOML service bundles, and compiles them into the system
    s6-rc database on package install (via postinst, or a postinst
    intercept at image construction time).

  * bbappends (enabled dynamically against openembedded-core) adding
    s6-rc service definitions for common daemons: bluez5, connman,
    dbus, openssh, wpa-supplicant and eudev, plus initscripts and
    sysvinit-inittab integration.

Dependencies
============

  URI: https://git.openembedded.org/openembedded-core
  branch: wrynose

Usage
=====

Add the layer to your build:

  bitbake-layers add-layer meta-s6

Then select the init manager in your distro configuration, either:

  require conf/distro/include/init-manager-s6-linux-init-s6-rc.inc

for s6-linux-init with s6-rc as the service manager, or:

  require conf/distro/include/init-manager-s6-linux-init-sysvinit-rc.inc

for s6-linux-init running traditional sysvinit rc scripts.

The same choice is available as a configuration fragment, which is
usually more convenient than editing a distro configuration:

  bitbake-config-build enable-fragment meta-s6/init-s6-rc

or meta-s6/init-sysvinit-rc.

Managing services on the target
===============================

Service definitions are installed by packages into the store in
/usr/share/s6/sources; /etc/s6/sources is left empty for local
definitions. The database booted from is compiled at image
construction time into /etc/s6-rc/compiled, and which services start at
boot is decided by the flag-essential and flag-recommended markers in
the definitions themselves, not by editing a bundle.

The s6-rc repository in /var/lib/s6-rc/repository, which the s6 set and
s6 repository commands work on, is built as part of the image, and the
database booted from is the set committed there, so s6 set and s6
repository work on a freshly flashed image and what boots is what
s6-rc-set-commit produced. Installing a package which ships service
definitions syncs the repository and installs the new set in the same
way.

docs/service-definitions.md describes the TOML format service
definitions are written in, and how it maps onto what s6-rc reads.

To change which services start at boot, work on the current set and
install it:

  s6 set enable <service>
  s6 set apply

s6 set apply commits the working set and makes it live; the boot
database is updated at the same time, so the change survives a reboot.

Testing under qemu
==================

meta-s6-qemu.conf.json is a bitbake-setup configuration which builds the
layer for qemu machines, using the poky distro and the Yocto Project's
prebuilt sstate mirror:

  bitbake-setup init https://raw.githubusercontent.com/akiernan/meta-s6/main/meta-s6-qemu.conf.json

Choose a configuration when prompted (or pass it on the command line):
each qemu machine is available with either of the layer's init flavours,
s6-rc or sysvinit-rc service management. Then:

  source <setup-dir>/build/init-build-env
  bitbake core-image-minimal
  runqemu nographic

Log in as root (no password) and inspect the supervision tree with
s6-rc -a list, s6-svstat, etc.

